WEBVTT

00:00:00.159 --> 00:00:04.497
This JSON record is self-describing because the keys tell us what each value represents.

00:00:04.497 --> 00:00:07.042
But it is not a flat table.

00:00:07.042 --> 00:00:11.882
Heart rate is nested inside metrics, and latitude and longitude are nested inside location.

00:00:11.882 --> 00:00:14.812
A second record could add oxygen saturation or omit sleep hours.

00:00:14.812 --> 00:00:18.842
Before analysis, we often flatten the JSON into columns.

00:00:18.842 --> 00:00:20.592
But flattening is only a structural step.

00:00:20.592 --> 00:00:25.897
We still need to understand units, missingness, and the analytical meaning of the extracted fields.

00:00:25.897 --> 00:00:29.447
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:29.447 --> 00:00:34.112
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:34.112 --> 00:00:36.587
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:36.587 --> 00:00:41.612
In a real setting such as an e-commerce team preparing customer, purchase, and web-event data,

00:00:41.612 --> 00:00:48.002
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:48.002 --> 00:00:48.352
before discussing syntax.

00:00:48.352 --> 00:00:53.440
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
